Whilst the writings of Leontius are somewhat unreliable, Mango has concluded
that the Life of John the Almsgiver‘can be used with some caution as a source for social and economic
history’;

All they do establish is that Apion employees had ﬁscal
obligations, not that no other sort of obligation was involved in the payments exacted by the
estate’;

N ev-
ertheless, Johnson and West felt able to conclude that ‘Justinian was evidently not worried by the
growth of patrocinium’;

As viewed
from the provinces, the ﬁgure of the great landowner bulked ever larger,
the direct authority of the emperor receded ever further from sight.

In trying cases he thought that it was a demonstration of his piety if any man
for allegedly religious purposes, in seizing something that did not belong to him,
having won the case, would go away scot-free.

In his con-
stitution on the province of Phoenecia Libanensis, for example, Justinian
demanded that the governor restrain the ‘households of the mighty’;
